AD</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">classic_change</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">classic_change</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	One day, I tried to prepare a full stock list for my kiosk, but my hands shook from skipping breakfast and feeling nervous. How could I focus when my head felt so heavy? I didn’t understand why, until a friend passing by the shop noticed me. 'Kendi, si you eat something first?' she asked. But I shrugged, thinking maybe it was just stress or hunger that would pass. In my heart, I began to worry about the big county market day coming soon. How would I manage with bigger orders if I could not even write a small list?\n\nLater, I visited the clinic for my insulin. The nurse, Mary—a kind woman with a big laugh—sat me down. She listened quietly when I spoke about my busy mornings. Then she explained, 'Kendi, your body needs fuel before work, even simple uji or boiled sweet potatoes. Try make three meals a day. Use sukuma wiki, nduma, omena, or maize if that’s what you have. And don’t forget to drink water.' I didn’t say much, just nodded, thinking it all sounded too simple to really help.\n\nThe next morning, I tried her advice. I boiled water for uji, sliced one tomato, and steamed sukuma wiki for lunch. It felt strange and slow, and I kept checking the time, anxious about opening late. My hands were used to just grabbing anything quick, and I nearly forgot my medicine. By midday, I sat behind my counter, feeling a bit restless. 'Can I really do this every day?' I wondered. But memories of past struggles pushed me to keep going, especially with that big market day just around the corner.\n\nOver a few weeks, I noticed small changes. My energy rose, my mood lightened, and I no longer dreaded mornings. When market day arrived, I managed orders, counted change with a steady hand, and even joked with customers. My fears faded. I realised following simple, local meal ideas made a big difference. Now, when the clinic nurse asks, 'How are you, Kendi?' I smile—for the first time in a long time, truly hopeful for more healthy days ahead.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9ff693fb-a872-4b87-832b-972038a21c8e_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9ff693fb-a872-4b87-832b-972038a21c8e_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9ff693fb-a872-4b87-832b-972038a21c8e_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9ff693fb-a872-4b87-