quot;,\n  &quot;What culturally appropriate, gluten-free recipes are common in the Kikuyu community for people with diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can a tea farmer in the Kiambu area balance farm work with maintaining a healthy lifestyle for diabetes management?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any faith-based (Muslim) health resources or counseling services in the Kiambu region for people managing Type 2 diabetes alone?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are the risks of combining insulin, metformin, and traditional herbal remedies while living in Kenya?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can I use storytelling in the Kikuyu tradition as a tool for managing stress and supporting diabetes care?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">exercise</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">5f802b45-004a-4936-8e10-8618cc60e1d7</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">independent</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">BAD</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">classic_change</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">classic_change</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Have you ever woken up and felt like your body just didn’t want to move? That was me, Wanjiku, on another early morning at my small tea shamba in Kiambu. I ate my usual breakfast—just a cup of strong sweet tea and two mandazi—to start the day. I was meant to weed just a small part of my tea bushes before the sun got too hot. But, haiya, my energy ran out quickly! My legs felt heavy, and I sat under a mugumo tree, wondering, did I do something wrong with my eating or medicine?\n\nOne day when I went for my insulin at the local clinic, Nurse Nyambura noticed how tired I looked. She greeted me and said, 'Wanjiku, maybe you are feeling low because you missed your proper breakfast, or you didn’t stretch your legs enough before work. Even small walks keep your blood sugar balanced, and remember your medication! Would you like me to help you make a plan for your mornings?' At first, I did not want to change what felt so normal to me. I wondered if these walks were truly for people like me. But nurse Nyambura explained, 'Even twenty minutes of simple movement helps your body use insulin better. You can start slowly. And halal breakfasts—like a boiled egg, sweet potatoes, and a little fruit—give you lasting strength.' Her caring voice made it sound so easy, so I decided to listen.\n\nSo, the next day, I tried Nurse Nyambura’s advice. I boiled some sweet potatoes and an egg for breakfast and took a slow walk near the tea rows before getting to work. It felt a bit strange—was it really necessary, or am I just wasting time? I tried to weed the bushes again, but halfway through, I was a bit tired, and old habits whispered, 'Just go fast, skip your walk tomorrow.' Still, I remembered that soon I had to prepare a big order for a local buyer. Could I finish it if I kept feeling so tired? I told myself, Wanjiku, just keep going, a little every day.\n\nDay by day, my body started to enjoy the new routine. My legs became lighter, and I didn’t get that heavy tiredness so quickly anymore. When the big order came, I managed to harvest more tea leaves than before and felt proud as I packed them for the market. Looking back, I realise those small steps—eating well, moving a bit each morning, and not skipping my medicine—made a big difference. Now, I feel hopeful about each new day, and if exercise helps my blood sugar and energy, why not continue? Maybe tomorrow, I will even invite a neighbour to join me for a walk.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5f802b45-004a-4936-8e10-8618cc60e1d7_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5f802b45-004a-4936-8e10-8618cc60e1d7_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5f802b45-004a-4936-8e10-8618cc60e1d7_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5f802b45-004a-4936-8e10-8618cc60e1d7_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5f802b45-004a-4936-8e10-8618cc60e1d7_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5f802b45-004a-4936-8e10-8618cc60e1d7_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5f802b45-004a-4936-8e10-8618cc60e1d7_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5f802b45-004a-4936-8e10-8618cc60e1d7_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	independent	2026-03-01 15:11:09.518375+00	\N
