 stay active and manage diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any local reading or social clubs in rural Kirinyaga for women where I can meet others and reduce feelings of isolation?&quot;,\n  &quot;Which types of physical activities are culturally appropriate and practical for a female coffee farmer with diabetes in the Kikuyu community?&quot;,\n  &quot;What advice do local clinics in Kirinyaga give about combining lifestyle changes and traditional medicine for diabetes management?&quot;,\n  &quot;Is it possible to find affordable, healthy vegetarian food options at rural markets in small Kirinyaga communities?&quot;,\n  &quot;How does regular physical activity, such as farm work and walking, impact blood sugar levels for middle-aged Kikuyu women with diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there faith-based (African Independent Churches) health or wellness programs for women with hypertension and diabetes in Kirinyaga?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">exercise</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">771a4ceb-d6c1-416b-9b0c-9bb0e4c93a45</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">overlapped</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Let me tell you, being a coffee farmer in this Kirinyaga countryside is not a joke—early mornings, plenty of mud, and work that never ends. Sometimes, after the first cock crows, I grab only a sweet potato and mug of tea, then rush to weed a small patch near the banana trees. One morning, I felt sluggish, but ignored the feeling. Eh, can you imagine? Before lunch, I grew dizzy and sat under the jacaranda for too long—the weeding unfinished, grass everywhere. Isn't it funny how sometimes I forget that my body needs more than just energy to work? I blamed old age, not knowing it was my blood sugar playing games.\n\nThe next clinic day, Nurse Kathambi came for her usual visit. I tried to laugh off my tiredness, saying, "Si nimezeeka (I'm just getting old), nurse." She shook her head, smiled that motherly smile and said, "Wanjiku, diabetes is not age, ni mwili tu. Remember your greens, some sukuma with githeri, and walk after breakfast, not with an empty stomach. Even your coffee weeding counts as exercise, so don't skip food." At first, I thought, do I have to change everything? But her words made sense—she even helped me plan what I could cook with my small kitchen garden.\n\nI started small, waking up earlier to slice pumpkins for mukimo, adding sukuma and green maize for lunch. The first week, I kept forgetting—sometimes I drank tea only, then felt faint while preparing seedlings. I worried: how do I fit all this health talk into my busy day? But with the big coffee harvest coming, I remembered Nurse Kathambi's advice. I forced myself to eat more greens before chores and to walk slow, not rush. I kept telling myself, if I finish the harvest strong, I can earn enough for the market and clinic visit.\n\nBy the time harvest arrived, I felt lighter—still tired, but the dizziness came less. My sugar was steadier. The day I finished picking the last coffee basket without stopping to catch my breath, I wanted to celebrate! Do you know that feeling of finishing your work, all by yourself? I started greeting my neighbors on the road and even joined the Sunday women's group for a walk. Slowly, I started to believe: if I keep mixing my work, food, and faith, maybe I can get even stronger. I am thinking now, what else can I change for next season?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/771a4ceb-d6c1-416b-9b0c-9bb0e4c93a45_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/771a4ceb-d6c1-416b-9b0c-9bb0e4c93a45_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/771a4ceb-d6c1-416b-9b0c-9bb0e4c93a45_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/771a4ceb-d6c1-416b-9b0c-9bb0e4c93a45_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/771a4ceb-d6c1-416b-9b0c-9bb0e4c93a45_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/771a4ceb-d6c1-416b-9b0c-9bb0e4c93a45_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/771a4ceb-d6c1-416b-9b0c-9bb0e4c93a45_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/771a4ceb-d6c1-416b-9b0c-9bb0e4c93a45_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-01 15:11:12.476143+00	\N
