 and suitable for diabetes, and where are they available in local shops or markets?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are effective strategies for balancing shop owner duties and regular meal/snack timing for blood sugar control in type 2 diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there recommendations from Kiambu healthcare clinics on healthy vegan snacks and portion sizes for diabetic patients?&quot;,\n  &quot;How do other young, female business owners in Kiambu manage healthy eating for diabetes, especially when socializing with family and friends?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are some healthy, quick vegan snacks I can prepare and bring to my shop without refrigeration in the Kenyan suburban climate?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">diet</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">1d4cb11a-9902-4566-ad12-4ab9a8f77397</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">control</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	It's funny, you know, how I always say 'Wanjiku, you know best,' but every day I find myself grabbing whatever is nearest when customers slow down. I eat banana fritters or a handful of popcorn from the street vendor right outside my duka. One morning, I was determined to take stock before the lunch crowd, but halfway through, my hands started shaking, and my forehead was wet with sweat. How can I run a business if my body doesn't cooperate? I had no clue that snacking at random, without thinking, was leaving me low on energy just when I needed it most.\n\nA week later, a nurse from Kiambu clinic passed by my shop for her groceries and saw me looking tired and cranky. She asked, 'Wanjiku, when do you eat? What snacks do you keep?' I shrugged, feeling a bit stubborn, not sure if she could really help. But she told me simple things—'Try roasted groundnuts or a small Githeri cup between meals, banana with some nuts, or sweet potatoes roasted at home. Just small, smart snacks three to four hours apart, and always bring water. Skipping snacks is fine too, but only if you’re not hungry. Listen to your body, jameni.' That sounded doable and even familiar, but I doubted if it would change anything. Still, she wrote me a list and said, 'One new habit every week is enough.'\n\nSo the next day I tried packing a small container of roasted peanuts and a few slices of roasted sweet potato. As the lunchtime rush hit, I wanted to reach for the oily mandazi instead, but I remembered the nurse’s advice and forced myself to grab the peanuts. It was hard, I won’t lie—old habits itch like a mosquito bite, right? Around four o’clock, my sister passed by and laughed, teasing me for nibbling on veggies like a rabbit. I almost gave up, thinking, 'What difference does it make?' But I remembered, tomorrow I had to do inventory for my shop and couldn’t afford to feel lousy again.\n\nWith each day, things got a little easier. My energy didn't crash before closing and, one Saturday, I finished my monthly sales report in record time. I felt proud and strong—like maybe I could trust my body again, just from changing my snacks and my attitude a little bit. My family noticed too, and suddenly my sister was asking for my sweet potato recipe. Maybe making these small choices wasn’t such a waste of time after all. Now I’m thinking, what if I actually keep this up? Who knows what more I could do for myself and my business.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1d4cb11a-9902-4566-ad12-4ab9a8f77397_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/s