e sugar seemed to drain my strength instead of giving energy. I could barely keep my thoughts straight, and before I knew it, I’d missed the deadline to send the results to the department head. Had my food and tiredness made that small job harder? I shook my head, wondering why simple things now felt so heavy.\n\nWhen I went to the clinic later that week for my check up, the nurse, Lydia, greeted me with a warm smile. 'Wambui, how have you been?' she asked as she checked my blood sugar. I hesitated—should I admit how tired I’ve been? Lydia nodded, understanding, and told me, 'You know, Mama, a little walking every day can help. Even in the city, just around your building or in the courtyard. It keeps your body awake, helps your blood sugar, and fresh air is good for your mind.' I sighed, thinking about my busy routine, but Lydia left me with these simple words: 'Start with ten minutes, even inside your house. Small steps lead to better health. And don’t wait to be strong—walking makes you strong.' It sounded possible…maybe even for me.\n\nThe next morning, I made up my mind to try Lydia’s advice. I put on my flat shoes and walked around our apartment corridor, even though I felt a little silly at first. My joints complained and my legs dragged, but I remembered I still had to prepare a lecture for the upcoming parent–student open day. Already, I felt like giving up, especially when my neighbour met me and asked why I was walking and not taking my tea as usual. I almost turned back, but I pushed on—if I could not finish small walks, how would I stand in front of all those parents next week?\n\nEach day, I walked just a little bit farther, sometimes laughing with my grandchildren when they joined me, other times praying quietly for God’s strength. My body began to feel lighter, and even my mornings looked brighter. The open day arrived, and standing at the front of the hall with my notes in hand, I realized I was not gasping for breath or losing focus. Instead, I spoke clearly, smiled, and even walked among the tables to chat with parents. The satisfaction warmed my heart. I thought, if walking did this, what else could small steps do? I left that day with hope—and a promise to myself to keep moving.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/734a6869-029f-40f4-8bd9-e2dcd8115ad0_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/734a6869-029f-40f4-8bd9-e2dcd8115ad0_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/734a6869-029f-40f4-8bd9-e2dcd8115ad0_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/734a6869-029f-40f4-8bd9-e2dcd8115ad0_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/734a6869-029f-40f4-8bd9-e2dcd8115ad0_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/734a6869-029f-40f4-8bd9-e2dcd8115ad0_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/734a6869-029f-40f4-8bd9-e2dcd8115ad0_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/734a6869-029f-40f4-8bd9-e2dcd8115ad0_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-01 15:11:12.713114+00	\N
