ts or snacks can I eat as a diabetic that wonâ€™t spike my blood glucose?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are some easy-to-make, diabetes-friendly traditional Kenyan recipes for someone with my schedule as a working adult?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can I balance my insulin and oral medication routines with my work schedule as an engineer in a Kiambu urban setting?&quot;,\n  &quot;Can you recommend books or reading materials about living well with type 2 diabetes that are relevant for Kenyan women?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any local healthcare clinics in Kiambu with diabetes education or wellness programs tailored for women without dietary restrictions?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">exercise</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">789484bf-ed1f-4981-add9-07ba55551235</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">independent</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">BAD</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">crisis_avoidance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">crisis_avoidance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every morning, as the sun peeks through the curtains of my modest flat in Parklands, I rush a quick cup of chai without sugar and gulp down my metformin with a bite of brown toast. Then off I go, squeezing into a packed matatu, worried I’ll be late at my job in the small office nearby. One day last week, my boss asked me to finish the monthly report quickly. I felt dizzy, my hands shook a little—aigaa, why now? I’d skipped my midmorning snack, thinking I’d be faster, but my blood sugar dropped and I couldn’t even type. The report slipped, and my supervisor noticed I looked pale and confused. Isn’t it funny how small mistakes can grow from just rushing through life and ignoring the body?\n\nLater that day, a friendly nurse called Naomi, at the clinic downstairs, pulled me aside when she saw I wasn’t myself. She spoke gently, ‘Wanjiku, my sister, your sugars need regular food and some walks. It helps your body use insulin — even ten minutes at lunch, just downstairs, can help keep you sharp for work. Next time you feel low, eat your snack or take a brisk walk. Eh, don’t fear. Try it tomorrow. Start small, like you’re fetching water from the tap.’ I wanted to argue, but her advice fit things I could actually do, not just big promises.\n\nThe next day, I packed boiled sweet potatoes and a banana instead of skipping my snack. After emails, I tried walking around the block, but I felt silly as I worried other shopkeepers were watching me. By midday, I almost forgot my snack when work got busy, and felt the same tiredness coming back. But then I remembered: bigger client assignment coming next week. I forced myself to take a break and eat before it got worse. It’s hard to change habits when pressure calls, but I needed to prove—I could do better.\n\nAfter a week of trying, it started feeling easier. Walking with a colleague made me laugh and feel less tense, and my blood sugars stopped swinging so wildly. When the big client project arrived, I stayed focused and finished with time to spare. My boss smiled and said, ‘Wanjiku, you’re shining these days!’ Now I see—taking small steps to look after my health gives me strength to handle work and anything else. Who knew that a walk, a snack, and good choices could make a difference? Next, I want to join the weekend walk at Uhuru Park. Maybe I’m stronger than I thought.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/789484bf-ed1f-4981-add9-07ba55551235_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/789484bf-ed1f-4981-add9-07ba55551235_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/789484bf-ed1f-4981-add9-07ba55551235_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/789484bf-ed1f-4981-add9-07ba55551235_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/789484bf-ed1f-4981-add9-07ba55551235_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/789484bf-ed1f-4981-add9-07ba55551235_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/789484bf-ed1f-4981-add9-07ba55551235_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/789484bf-ed1f-4981-add9-07ba55551235_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	independent	2026-03-01 15:11:08.228553+00	\N
