<tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My days start early in Kiambu, running around the flats to check on tenants and fix small problems. My breakfast is usually githeri and tea, maybe a quick chapati if it’s Friday and I feel a bit fancy. Honestly, I always thought that being busy on my feet was enough exercise. So when the water pump broke this week, I fixed it myself, up and down the stairs a hundred times, sweating, a bit dizzy but I told myself, "Wanjiku, you are working hard, so you are healthy!" But later, my sugar dropped, and I nearly fainted outside the storage room. Did it ever cross my mind to plan my exercise – or even snack before such chores? Not at all. I just kept moving – not realising my diabetes is not only about being active, but how I am active.\n\nAfter that scare, my sister dragged me to the local clinic – Two Feet Clinic. The nurse was very kind, speaking Kikuyu and English just to be sure I got the message! She said, "Walking is good, Wanjiku, but you need a structured plan – not just running during work. Join a group at FitSpot or Extrim; try Zumba, strength, or yoga. It is not about how busy, it’s how you move and balance it with your medications, meals and snacks." Eh, honestly? I wanted to argue – I’m Kikuyu and naturally strong, why change now? But she smiled, "You want to be fit for your tenants and family, right? Do it together, even as a story you’ll share!" It sounded so simple but different from my routine.\n\nThe next week, I tried to follow her advice. I even signed up for a walking club at Karura Forest and joined Zumba at FitSpot Gym. Walking in a straight line, not dodging tenants or counting rent, felt very odd! Sometimes my legs were sore, and I missed smoking with friends after work, or rushed and forgot to bring a healthy snack. One afternoon my energy dropped bad – I nearly left the Zumba class, but then I remembered, the block’s annual inspection was this Saturday. I had to be strong! No fainting in front of tenants again.\n\nSo I pushed on. Each day, things got easier. My sugar readings were better, and my body felt lighter. When inspection day came, not only did I finish the checks, I even joined the caretakers for a Kibuyu lunch of githeri and sukuma, sharing my exercise stories. My family cheered me on, and even my tenants noticed I climbed stairs without panting. Now, walking is just the start. Maybe next month, I’ll try yoga or bring my friends along. Why stop here when I can become even better? Let me see how far this new habit can take me.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9d2d4ce3-73ba-4010-a182-e34822dc9641_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9d2d4ce3-73ba-4010-a182-e34822dc9641_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9d2d4ce3-73ba-4010-a182-e34822dc9641_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9d2d4ce3-73ba-4010-a182-e34822dc9641_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9d2d4ce3-73ba-4010-a182-e34822dc9641_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9d2d4ce3-73ba-4010-a182-e34822dc9641_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9d2d4ce3-73ba-4010-a182-e34822dc9641_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9d2d4ce3-73ba-4010-a182-e34822dc9641_3.mp3}	Can I just walk for exercise, or should I do other types too?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-01 15:11:11.779688+00	\N
