s in northern Nigeria?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can I access diabetes-friendly gluten-free ingredients in suburban Kano markets near rice farms?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are effective meal prep strategies for busy rice farmers in northern Nigerian suburbs aiming to avoid late-night eating?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there successful stories or resources for Hausa Muslim men who balance diabetes management and farming work near Kano?&quot;,\n  &quot;Is fasting during Ramadan safe for a 35-44 year old Hausa man with type 2 diabetes who smokes and takes both insulin and metformin?&quot;,\n  &quot;How does weekly socializing with friends and family in rural Kano affect stress and blood sugar management for diabetic rice farmers?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">exercise</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">27953e80-fb03-4be3-8aa1-5d017f0b6518</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">overlapped</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Akira Watanabe, and almost every morning, just after fajr prayer, you’ll find me stretching my arms out towards the sky as the sun rises behind the rice paddies near Kano. As a rice farmer, life is busy, wallahi, every day is almost the same. I wake up, eat whatever leftover tuwo shinkafa or masa is around, grab a quick cup of tea, and march straight to the fields. Who has time to think about exercise or special meals if the rice won’t plant itself, eh? One Wednesday, my smallest field by the road needed weeding before a family friend’s visit. "Ah, this is small work," I thought, so I skipped my breakfast, smoked a little to calm my nerves, and tried to do it fast. Halfway, my arms started feeling heavy, sweat poured, and I sat under a tree, shaky and too tired to finish. I wondered, is it age or just poor luck?\n\nLater, my sister Fatima noticed me restless on the veranda and quickly dragged me to the local hospital. To be honest, I didn’t want to go, but Fatima had her mind made up, and who can argue with sisters? There in the cool clinic, Nurse Amina checked my blood sugar and told me, ‘Akira, this work needs food and timing. Eat early, move in the morning, and don’t skip meals, even for small work. If you keep to these, your head will clear, and your strength will last longer. Also, finish your dinner before sunset—night eating is not helping you!’ She gave me a meal plan using tuwo shinkafa, beans, and fresh leaf soup—foods I already know! I nodded, though my heart said, can simple things really change this feeling?\n\nI tried to do as nurse Amina said. The next day, I made sure to eat breakfast—rice and steamed greens—before walking to the paddies. I swapped late-night snacking for a cup of koko, just as the sun was setting. But, ah, breaking old habits isn’t easy—by afternoon, my legs felt lazy and I almost wanted to sit again, but a memory of last time pushed me on. The big ‘Saturday Community Weeding’ was coming up, with all the elders watching. I told myself, Akira, just keep going, at least until then.\n\nAs the days passed, eating early and planning my meals didn’t feel so strange. My hands stopped shaking, and I even joked with my neighbours while working. When Saturday came, I led the group through thick grass, strong from start to finish under the sun. I felt proud, not just for the work, but for not giving up on myself. Maybe, if little changes like eating and moving right can help so much, I’m ready to try more. Perhaps my story will make my friends laugh—and listen—and we can all work and live better together.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/27953e80-fb03-4be3-8aa1-5d017f0b6518_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/27953e80-fb03-4be3-8aa1-5d017f0b6518_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/27953e80-fb03-4be3-8aa1-5d017f0b6518_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/27953e80-fb03-4be3-8aa1-5d017f0b6518_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/27953e80-fb03-4be3-8aa1-5d017f0b6518_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/27953e80-fb03-4be3-8aa1-5d017f0b6518_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/27953e80-fb03-4be3-8aa1-5d017f0b6518_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/27953e80-fb03-4be3-8aa1-5d017f0b6518_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	male	overlapped	2026-02-22 22:58:59.553183+00	\N
