ment, and are these safe?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are the best ways to involve my family in supporting my diabetes care and physical activity routines in an urban Kenyan context?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there local fitness centers or community exercise programs in Kiambu that cater to women with diabetes and provide guidance in English or Kikuyu?&quot;,\n  &quot;How does access to fresh fruits and vegetables in Kiambuâ€™s urban markets impact healthy eating options for shop owners with diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there government or NGO-funded resources in Kiambu city to help young women entrepreneurs manage diabetes alongside their business?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are effective motivation strategies for busy working women in Kenya to start exercising when they have type 2 diabetes but currently have zero activity days per week?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">exercise</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">8538621f-9678-4acf-af51-9eecff4c00f4</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">overlapped</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Eh, let me tell you – some days, running my shop here in Kiambu town just leaves me tired for nothing. I open early, wipe the shelves, arrange stock, and grab some mukimo or githeri left over from last night to eat with my tea, you know? I always think, "Wanjiku, you still have your medicine, so you are alright." But hapa, that Thursday, I was supposed to help a neighbour print school forms inside my shop. My knees felt heavy, my sugar was just low, and by the time she came, the power had tripped and I did not even have strength to fix it. See, I forgot to eat at the right time and didn’t move around. It was a simple thing, but I just sat, feeling defeated, watching her walk away shaking her head. How can a strong lady like me be weaker than a small task?\n\nThe next afternoon, my friend Mary – she works at the local clinic – popped in carrying her big brown bag. She saw me sitting slumped behind the counter, notebook in hand but no energy. She asked, "Wanjiku, are you eating before work? Are you walking at all?" I just shrugged and told her, "Ai, Mary, sometimes I even forget to move, yet I feel tired every day." Mary explained, "My friend, even short walks on your street can wake up your body, help your medicine work better, and give you strength. Try just walking briskly to the end of the road and back, ten minutes after lunch. It clears your mind, and is good for your blood sugar – you will even handle bigger things." I smiled, but I thought, is it really that easy for me? Still, I promised to try.\n\nThe next day, after closing for lunch, I put on my old sneakers and locked the shop. I started my ten minutes, arms swinging, but honestly, I felt breathless after only a few minutes. A neighbour asked, "Wanjiku, why are you walking like you are chasing a thief?" We both laughed and I wanted to give up. But I remembered – next week I’m hosting my church group in the shop, and I must be strong to serve everyone. So I wiped the sweat, rested by a tree for a moment, and finished my walk, even though my legs grumbled all the way home.\n\nIt took me three more walks to stop feeling like a broken wheel. My energy came back little by little, and I learned to pack a small snack to eat before heading out. By the time my church group arrived, I was moving around with a smile, arranging seats, helping serve food – not just watching. I even invited Mary to join us, and she teased me about my ‘big shop energy’. Now I know – these small changes can help me do more than I ever thought. Maybe next month, I will even join the community walk in town. Who would have guessed, exercise helps my sugar and my confidence at the same time?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8538621f-9678-4acf-af51-9eecff4c00f4_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8538621f-9678-4acf-af51-9eecff4c00f4_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8538621f-9678-4acf-af51-9eecff4c00f4_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8538621f-9678-4acf-af51-9eecff4c00f4_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/8538621f-9678-4acf-af51-9eecff4c00f4_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/8538621f-9678-4acf-af51-9eecff4c00f4_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/8538621f-9678-4acf-af51-9eecff4c00f4_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/8538621f-9678-4acf-af51-9eecff4c00f4_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-01 15:11:09.209146+00	\N
