n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">occupation</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">daily_routine</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">hobbies</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe)</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">activities</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_conditions</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Hypertension (high blood pressure)</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">medication</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">healthcare_resources</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">healthcare_access</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">healthcare_frequency</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">culture</td>\n                <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">t2d_status</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">tobacco</td>\n                <td class="p-2">No</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">alcohol</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">physical_activity</td>\n                <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">diet_restrictions</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Vegetarian</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">support_network</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Question</td>\n                <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">generated_queries</td>\n                <td class="p-2">[\n  &quot;What are some traditional isiXhosa vegetarian recipes suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes and hypertension living in the Eastern Cape rural areas?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can a 55–64 year old male teacher in rural Eastern Cape find community exercise groups or traditional dancing classes like umxhentso?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can I manage diabetes and high blood pressure as a vegetarian with limited access to healthy foods in my small rural community?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any mobile health clinics or outreach programmes in the rural Eastern Cape that support diabetes management for older adults?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are the recommended exercise routines for men over 55 with hypertension and diabetes who haven&#x27;t been physically active recently?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can I balance participating in traditional dancing (intlombe) with managing my blood sugar and blood pressure levels?&quot;,\n  &quot;What culturally relevant herbal or traditional medicines do isiXhosa communities use for managing diabetes, and are they safe to use with insulin and metformin?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can I access affordable vegetarian food options in rural areas of the Eastern Cape with limited markets?&quot;,\n  &quot;What is the impact of daily alcohol consumption on diabetes and hypertension for middle-aged isiXhosa men in rural South Africa?&quot;,\n  &quot;How often should someone with type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure attend local healthcare clinics in rural Eastern Cape for check-ups?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there specific Baptist church support groups in the Eastern Cape for people managing chronic illnesses like diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;What exercises can be done safely indoors at home for older adults with diabetes who have limited outdoor facilities in rural South Africa?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">diet</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">94b8ac56-333c-4fd2-bb44-a8dc4aacb9e7</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">overlapped</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every day I, Siyabulela Khumalo, wake up before sunrise to prepare for school in our small village in the Eastern Cape. My home is a simple rondavel with a thatched roof, walls cold in the morning but filled with the sounds of birds, goats, and sometimes my own humming as I make umngqusho with beans for breakfast. I am vegetarian because of my health, but to be honest, our food options here are not many. Sometimes, after a long day of teaching, marking books, and listening to learners' stories, I don't eat supper until it’s almost bedtime. Funny thing, I wanted to finish marking all the test papers last week, but after I ate late, I started feeling tired and dizzy. Did I ever think about how that affects my diabetes? Eh, not really. The work didn’t get done. I went to bed with papers still on the table, wondering why I felt so slow and heavy.\n\nTwo days later, Nurse Nontle from the clinic visited our school for a health talk. I almost wanted to slip out the back, but when she asked about healthy habits for diabetes and high blood pressure, I made a noise in my throat. She looked at me – 'Siyabulela, eating late at night can push your blood sugar too high and make you feel tired the next day,' she said. She advised: 'Try and eat supper earlier, maybe around six o’clock, and even simple food like imifino or samp with beans is good. If you are hungry later, have a small fruit or a glass of amasi. This way, your body rests well, and you wake up fresh for your many books.'\n\nI tried to follow her advice the next evening. I cooked earlier and sat down, but my hands itched to finish school paperwork, and by the time I was done, the sun had set and I almost forgot to eat. The new routine was not easy. Old habits, you know? I ended up nibbling only a little later, feeling like something was off – not just my sugar, but my whole plan. But the school principal had announced a big community traditional dance event, and as one of the cultural leaders, I was expected to organise and dance with the boys. That gave me reason to try again, thinking, can one meal really make this much difference?\n\nA week passed and I got better at planning meals and eating before dark, even if I had a peanut butter sandwich sometimes instead of cooked food. Slowly, my mornings grew lighter, I was less tired, and marking papers became easier. When the big cultural event arrived, I stood proud – danced with energy, and helped the learners remember our steps. My body felt lighter and my mind sharper. Who knew, just changing my supper time made me ready for much more life in my years at this age? Now, I even want to share what I learned with other teachers at the clinic’s next talk.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/94b8ac56-333c-4fd2-bb44-a8dc4aacb9e7_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/94b8ac56-333c-4fd2-bb44-a8dc4aacb9e7_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/94b8ac56-333c-4fd2-bb44-a8dc4aacb9e7_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/94b8ac56-333c-4fd2-bb44-a8dc4aacb9e7_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/94b8ac56-333c-4fd2-bb44-a8dc4aacb9e7_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/94b8ac56-333c-4fd2-bb44-a8dc4aacb9e7_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/94b8ac56-333c-4fd2-bb44-a8dc4aacb9e7_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/94b8ac56-333c-4fd2-bb44-a8dc4aacb9e7_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	male	overlapped	2026-02-22 23:02:41.744906+00	\N
