ss="p-2 font-bold">tobacco</td>\n                <td class="p-2">No</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">alcohol</td>\n                <td class="p-2">No</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">physical_activity</td>\n                <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">diet_restrictions</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Lactose-free</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">support_network</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Question</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">generated_queries</td>\n                <td class="p-2">[\n  &quot;What are some vegan isiXhosa dishes available in the Eastern Cape, specifically near suburban areas, that are suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes and hypertension?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can I find local support groups for female school teachers managing diabetes in Eastern Cape suburban communities?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there evening exercise classes or walking groups in suburban areas of Eastern Cape that cater to women with no physical impairments but low physical activity levels?&quot;,\n  &quot;How do traditional family gatherings in isiXhosa culture typically handle vegan food options, and what healthy dishes can I bring for celebrations given my dietary restrictions?&quot;,\n  &quot;What resources or programs are available at local clinics in the Eastern Cape for managing high blood pressure and diabetes together as a young adult female?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there tips specific to South African suburban lifestyles for avoiding late-night eating and controlling blood sugar levels?&quot;,\n  &quot;What lifestyle changes have helped isiXhosa women in Eastern Cape balance work (as teachers), family events, and chronic conditions like diabetes and hypertension?&quot;,\n  &quot;Is there a Christian (Zion Christian Church) health or wellness group in the Eastern Cape that supports young women with diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can I buy affordable vegan, diabetes-friendly food options near my area with easy access to healthy foods in the Eastern Cape suburbs?&quot;,\n  &quot;What dietary advice is available for people with type 2 diabetes who are vegan in South Africa, especially regarding staple isiXhosa foods?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there local government or clinic-led initiatives in the Eastern Cape that support physical activity for diabetic women with hypertension?&quot;,\n  &quot;How does cultural attitude within isiXhosa families in suburban Eastern Cape influence support for young women managing chronic health conditions like mine?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">exercise</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">f0169f86-a2ad-4864-848e-52825a4e054f</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">independent</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every weekday, nje, I wake up before the sun and pour all my energy into preparing lessons for my learners. I rush through breakfast, sometimes just a slice of brown bread with avocado—if I'm lucky, maybe some leftover imifino from last night. Does anyone really have time for a proper meal when the staff meeting starts at 7:30? By break-time, I feel flat—my sugar low, my head spinning, but still I carry my marking pile to class. Today, a simple task: decorate the bulletin board for the Mother Tongue Day celebration. I thought, 'This will be quick.' But halfway, my hands started trembling and my vision blurred. I just stood there, feeling silly—how do you explain to your principal you can't finish because of sugar? I guess I didn't really think how much diabetes could spoil small things at work.\n\nAfter the bell, I decided to walk to the local clinic. Sister Lindiwe welcomed me, smiling, and asked if I was managing my diabetes and blood pressure. I tried to brush off my tiredness, saying, 'Yoh, sisi, I just need to rest.' But she said gently, Zanele, 'You must move your body, even just a little. Walking after supper, making umngqusho with less salt, and eating more imifino will keep your sugar stable.' She showed me a colourful pamphlet about healthy vegan dishes and wrote down easy tips for me—reminding me not to skip meals, to control my stress by talking to family, and to join the new walking group at the library parking lot. I nodded, but honestly, I wondered: Can short walks and leafy greens really make a difference when my days are so full?\n\nThe next day, I tried to follow her advice. I cooked a big pot of umngqusho with sugar beans and made sure to take my lunch to school. After work, I felt lazy—my heart said 'rest.' But the flyer for the evening walking group was in my bag, and I remembered I still had to prepare the drama for the big school assembly next week. I forced myself outside, but my pace was slow and my legs felt heavy. With each step, I was ready to give up, but I told myself, 'If I don't do this now, how will I last when the school is full of parents and guests?' It wasn't easy. I struggled, breath short, but finally made it round the block—one small win.\n\nAfter a few days, things started to feel different. I joined the walking group twice in one week. I felt less tired during lessons, and the bulletin board looked beautiful by Friday. On assembly day, I stood in front of all those parents—confident, not shaky, with a bright voice. My family noticed too. My mom even asked for my recipe for butternut mash! Maybe exercise is just another lesson—I don't have to run, but if I keep moving and eating well, who knows? I might teach my own body new tricks—just like I teach my learners every day.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f0169f86-a2ad-4864-848e-52825a4e054f_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f0169f86-a2ad-4864-848e-52825a4e054f_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f0169f86-a2ad-4864-848e-52825a4e054f_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f0169f86-a2ad-4864-848e-52825a4e054f_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f0169f86-a2ad-4864-848e-52825a4e054f_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f0169f86-a2ad-4864-848e-52825a4e054f_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f0169f86-a2ad-4864-848e-52825a4e054f_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f0169f86-a2ad-4864-848e-52825a4e054f_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	independent	2026-02-22 23:02:43.063341+00	\N
