            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, every morning I wake up before the sun, pour some strong rooibos, and start my day with a slice of white bread with jam—quick and familiar. The bell rings at school, and there I am, rushing from class to class, sometimes just grabbing amagwinya from the tuckshop between lessons. Who has time to think about fancy meals when the children are waiting in the classroom? Just last Monday, I tried marking all my learners’ books at break. My energy crashed, my hands shook, and I couldn’t finish. Why does my body betray me like this, just when I want to be reliable for my class?\n\nLater that same week at the clinic, the nurse saw how tired I was and asked about my eating. I said ai, as long as there’s food, what must I complain for? She looked me in the eye and spoke gently: “Mama Nozuko, you work so hard—your food must work hard for you too. Try Xhosa favourites like umngqusho (samp and beans), steamed with little oil and more beans. Add leafy greens for lunch, and pumpkin mixed in with maize porridge for breakfast. Remember—low salt, focus on plants, and skip the fat cakes and sugar, they are fighting your health.” I wanted to roll my eyes but something in her soft tone stayed with me.\n\nThat weekend, I set beans to boil and chopped spinach, feeling more like I was playing at cooking than making our real food. The tastes were not the same as my old stews with chicken or beef. I missed my bread and felt hungry by mid-morning on Monday. I worried—if I can’t get this right, how will I survive exam month? But, as I packed umngqusho in my recycled ice-cream tub for school, I reminded myself that school reports were due, and I needed my full brain and body for the big staff meeting ahead.\n\nBy the second week, the shakes had stopped. At our staff meeting, my mind felt clear and my spirit lighter—could it be this food was working after all? I finished my work before others, surprising even myself. Now, instead of resisting, I’m talking with a friend about joining a walking group at the service centre. Maybe keeping my blood sugar steady can help me with more than just marking—maybe I can really feel lively for my class.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3a37be9d-eb52-41dc-8554-e2c74a18bdbe_0.png,https://prxquaevkggp