. I pushed on, thinking it would pass, but had to sit down. Can't a man just work, finish, and go home like always?\n\nA female clinic nurse, Zodwa, stands beside Bulelani Mhlontlo in a busy township clinic with green walls, fluorescent lighting. Nurse Zodwa, in a crisp blue uniform, reviews Bulelani’s health record while he sits upright on a worn bench, hands fidgeting on his knees, eyes cast slightly away.\n\nClinic nurse Zodwa, female, speaks softly to Bulelani Mhlontlo in a semi-private alcove. Background: faint echoes of clinic chatter, distant children’s cries. Zodwa's mid-range, calm voice alternates with Bulelani’s slower, uncertain responses. Slight pause after advice, Bulelani’s gentle sigh and reshuffling on the bench. Footsteps pass. Zodwa says, 'You can start with short walks before or after work, Bulelani. Bring a friend or pray as you walk. If you feel shaky, don’t push. Can you try that?'\n\nBack at home, I tried adding a short walk right after supper, just down the dusty street and back. Is it easy to change an old routine? No ways! Some evenings, I’d rather watch TV or join my cousin in the lounge, chatting about soccer. One night, I forgot my insulin and felt a bit tired again. But with the storeroom reorganisation still looming, I kept going, thinking—what if I end up in hospital?\n\nIt was slow going, but over two weeks, walking got easier. On the big reorganisation day, I felt less tired and my energy lasted. No dizzy spells this time! I could carry the boxes and even help another worker without stopping for a break. My boss smiled, and I knew things can change if you try. Now, I greet my neighbour on the walk each night, feeling proud. Maybe it’s not too late to take real care of myself, hey?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_3.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_4.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_3.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/55f756a2-4331-4c13-a370-b33f6f292099_4.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	male	independent	2026-02-22 23:02:42.15429+00	\N
