  <td class="p-2">No</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">alcohol</td>\n                <td class="p-2">No</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">physical_activity</td>\n                <td class="p-2">1–2 days</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">diet_restrictions</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">support_network</td>\n                <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Question</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">generated_queries</td>\n                <td class="p-2">[\n  &quot;What are cost-effective, lactose-free meal options suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes living in suburban areas of the Eastern Cape, South Africa?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can a young female teacher in the Eastern Cape incorporate traditional IsiXhosa dishes, like umxhentso foods, into a healthy diet for managing diabetes on a budget?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there local Eastern Cape support groups or resources specifically for Muslim women living with type 2 diabetes who have limited family or community support?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can I find affordable, healthy groceries and staples for diabetes management near suburban neighborhoods around Eastern Cape cities?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can I participate safely in traditional dancing activities, such as intlombe, with type 2 diabetes and limited weekly exercise—are there any local programs for young adults?&quot;,\n  &quot;What culturally relevant exercise routines are recommended for Xhosa Muslim women aged 18–24 managing diabetes, especially for those working full-time as teachers?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any government or community initiatives in the Eastern Cape that help subsidize healthy food costs for young adults with diabetes who live alone?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are some quick and healthy lunch ideas for a busy teacher with lactose intolerance and type 2 diabetes in South Africa, using local Eastern Cape ingredients?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can I honor Islamic dietary restrictions and IsiXhosa cultural food traditions while following a lactose-free, diabetes-friendly diet in the Eastern Cape?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there specific tips for balancing my medication (insulin and oral meds) with my daily teaching routine and physical activity as a young adult with diabetes in this area?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are the best places in suburban Eastern Cape to find lactose-free dairy alternatives that are budget-friendly and suitable for people with diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can someone my age living alone manage stress and emotional wellbeing as a Muslim Xhosa woman with diabetes in an urbanizing part of the Eastern Cape?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">exercise</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">0ed4e164-1088-4697-91d4-c3893dbfe3f8</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">consistency</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2">dee2c33c-3052-455f-b549-78b58b411a6c</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Sometimes, I wake up to the sound of my alarm and just want to hit snooze. I get up, grab two slices of white bread and sweet tea for breakfast, not even thinking if it's good for my blood sugar. My morning is busy with lesson plans. Just yesterday, I tried to help my Grade 1s cut paper for art time. But halfway through, I felt so tired, my hands started shaking, and I had to sit down. What happened? I didn’t eat a proper breakfast and I forgot my morning insulin. It made me feel a bit useless, you know? How can I teach if I can't even finish my work?\n\nWhen break time finally came, I went to the clinic near the school. Sister Nomhle, the nurse, saw I wasn't my cheerful self. She listened and just nodded. She said, 'Ayanda, you are young and strong, but even young teachers must look after themselves. E at slow-release foods like oats or pap and egg for breakfast, take your medicine, and move a little bit more in your day. Try walking with your learners after school or do your umxhentso dances at home. It is not about being perfect, but about trying.' She smiled and made everything sound simple. Can good health really start that easily?\n\nBack at home, I tried what Sister Nomhle suggested. The next morning, I made oats with apple and cinnamon, even though I missed amasi a bit. Taking my insulin felt weird to pause for, and when I walked with the kids after assembly I felt out of breath quickly. There was a small intlombe coming up on the weekend, and I kept thinking, how will I have any energy to dance if even walking feels hard? Sometimes it felt easier to go back to old habits, but I wanted to prove to myself – and my learners – that I could do it.\n\nBit by bit I got used to these new ways. Each day, I felt less tired after school and my sugar was coming right. When the intlombe came, I joined in and danced every step with the other women, laughing and singing. My skirt swirled, my cheeks were bright. I did not feel useless – I felt proud. So, maybe I can't always be perfect, but I saw that small changes do add up. Now, I actually look forward to those quick walks, my oats in the morning, and I know my blood sugar is not the boss of me anymore. What other things could I try next?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0ed4e164-1088-4697-91d4-c3893dbfe3f8_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0ed4e164-1088-4697-91d4-c3893dbfe3f8_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0ed4e164-1088-4697-91d4-c3893dbfe3f8_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0ed4e164-1088-4697-91d4-c3893dbfe3f8_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0ed4e164-1088-4697-91d4-c3893dbfe3f8_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0ed4e164-1088-4697-91d4-c3893dbfe3f8_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0ed4e164-1088-4697-91d4-c3893dbfe3f8_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0ed4e164-1088-4697-91d4-c3893dbfe3f8_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-02-22 23:02:42.49648+00	\N
