             <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, each morning I wake up in my flat in the city, thinking there’s enough time to do everything—I take my insulin jab, make a quick bowl of umngqusho and cup of strong rooibos, and then head straight out. But, truth be told, I always rush—and there’s hardly ever any exercise in the equation, except for running to the taxi rank if I’m late. Today, I couldn’t even finish the small marking task I had promised my colleagues because I felt tired halfway through the lesson—my mind just started drifting, and my legs felt heavy. I thought skipping breakfast walks during the week wouldn’t matter, but here I was, behind on my work and feeling a bit useless in front of my class. Isn’t it strange how the body lets you know what it needs, even when you pretend not to notice?\n\nLater that afternoon, I went to the local healthcare clinic to collect my insulin. The nurse, Sister Thandi, asked about my tiredness, and I shrugged it off—'It’s just teaching, you know?' She smiled gently and told me, 'Sipho, your body needs regular movement, just like it needs the right food and medicine. Aim for thirty minutes of walking or football, five times a week—even if you split into two sessions a day or have to do it before school and after school. You can even join lunchtime stretches in the staffroom or walk during your break. Activities you enjoy, like football, will help your sugar and your heart, especially with your high cholesterol. Start small, and listen to your body.'\n\nThe next morning, I tried waking up earlier to fit in a brisk walk before work. Yoh, it wasn’t easy! My pillow felt like it had extra gravity, and I nearly convinced myself to stay in bed. But I remembered I had to attend the big parent meeting at school next week, and I didn’t want to feel tired in front of the parents and principal again. Walking in the dawn, I felt awkward—like everyone was watching—but I kept going, even stopping once to catch my breath by the vendor with fresh guavas. I messed up my timing and arrived at school just as the bell rang, a bit sweaty, but I didn’t run out of energy during class this time.\n\nDay by day, the walk became less of a mission and more of a habit. My tiredness faded, and when the big school meeting arrived, I stood up with confidence, explaining the term plan and even throwing in a joke about our football results—everyone laughed, and I didn’t once worry about running out of steam. Now, I look forward to those walks, and after class, I’ve even started a small teachers' football game on Fridays. Who knew such a simple routine could give me back my energy and a little bit of joy? Exercise, it turns out, is the lesson I needed most.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/da237700-3e58-4d14-8750-860d51f4783a_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/da237700-3e58-4d14-8750-860d51f4783a_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/da237700-3e58-4d14-8750-860d51f4783a_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/da237700-3e58-4d14-8750-860d51f4783a_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/da237700-3e58-4d14-8750-860d51f4783a_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/da237700-3e58-4d14-8750-860d51f4783a_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/da237700-3e58-4d14-8750-860d51f4783a_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/da237700-3e58-4d14-8750-860d51f4783a_3.mp3}	How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?	exercise	male	overlapped	2026-02-22 23:02:42.848553+00	\N
