ss="p-2 font-bold">tobacco</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">alcohol</td>\n                <td class="p-2">No</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">physical_activity</td>\n                <td class="p-2">5–7 days</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">diet_restrictions</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Vegetarian</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">support_network</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Question</td>\n                <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">generated_queries</td>\n                <td class="p-2">[\n  &quot;What are some affordable gluten-free South African traditional foods, specifically from the IsiXhosa culture, that I can prepare in rural Eastern Cape with limited access to healthy foods?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where in rural Eastern Cape can male fish traders like me with type 2 diabetes buy low-cost, fresh produce suitable for a gluten-free diet?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can I modify traditional umxhentso or intlombe celebration meals to be both healthy for diabetes and gluten-free, while still keeping the authentic IsiXhosa taste?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are effective, budget-friendly meal prep tips for young adults living in rural Eastern Cape who have limited food options and need to manage diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any local community programs or government initiatives in the Eastern Cape that support healthy eating for rural residents with type 2 diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can high-intensity physical activity or exercise, like traditional Xhosa dancing, impact my blood sugar control as a regular smoker with type 2 diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are some simple, accessible recipes using fish (since I am a fish trader) that fit gluten-free and diabetic diets, using ingredients found in rural South Africa?&quot;,\n  &quot;Can I still enjoy indigenous foods like umphokoqo or imifino while following a gluten-free, diabetic-friendly diet in Eastern Cape?&quot;,\n  &quot;How do high tobacco use and regular exercise affect diabetes management in rural South African men aged 25-34?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can I find support groups for diabetic Xhosa men in rural Eastern Cape who want to quit smoking or learn more about healthy living?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there respected herbal or traditional medicines in the IsiXhosa community that safely complement insulin and oral diabetes medications?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are the best strategies for a working adult in rural Eastern Cape to educate family about affordable, gluten-free diet changes appropriate for type 2 diabetes?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">diet</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">93508d04-a273-4efe-93ec-133cccb836ff</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">control</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Lumko Ntloko, and every day I wake up early because I must get my fish stall ready by the roadside, here in our small Eastern Cape village. Sometimes, I come home late, feet tired and hands smelling of fish, and I just eat whatever leftover umngqusho or maize meal is in the pot. Most nights, I don’t think too much about when or how much I eat—food is food, right? Last week, I tried to pack the fish boxes for tomorrow before sleeping, but I started feeling dizzy and confused, dropping the cooler lid on my toe! Yho, how can a grown man mess up so small a job?\n\nThe next morning at the clinic, the nurse, Sister Thandeka, checked my sugar and shook her head. She said, 'Lumko, eating late at night is making your blood sugar too high. You feel tired and dizzy the next day, and it makes your diabetes harder to control.' I felt stubborn. 'But Sister, what must I do, when the day is long and food is not always there during the day?' She smiled and showed me easy ways to change: eat supper early, quick dishes like imifino or roasted pumpkin after work, and do not eat heavy maize late. Even tea with groundnuts is better than a big plate at midnight, she said. Her Xhosa words stayed in my head—simple, but I wondered if my belly would listen.\n\nThat night, I tried to eat earlier, but after traditional dancing and washing up, my hands reached for the leftover pap like before. My sister Nomsa shouted, 'Hayi, Lumko! Remember what the nurse said!' I grumbled but left the food and just took a small bowl of leafy imifino with a squeeze of lemon. Later, I tossed in bed, missing my usual late snack, but I knew tomorrow the big delivery was coming, so I had to try harder. Isn’t it strange how our bodies fight what is good for us?\n\nThe next morning, I woke up lighter, not dizzy, and went early to the riverside to pack my fish for the big Saturday market. My hands were steady, my mind clear. I even danced a bit by the scale, feeling proud. The market was a success. I sold all the fish and even shared simple food tips with another trader. It’s not easy to change, hey, but I see now that if I keep this up, maybe one day, even traditional celebrations will taste as sweet as victory when I look after my health.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/93508d04-a273-4efe-93ec-133cccb836ff_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/93508d04-a273-4efe-93ec-133cccb836ff_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/93508d04-a273-4efe-93ec-133cccb836ff_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/93508d04-a273-4efe-93ec-133cccb836ff_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/93508d04-a273-4efe-93ec-133cccb836ff_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/93508d04-a273-4efe-93ec-133cccb836ff_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/93508d04-a273-4efe-93ec-133cccb836ff_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/93508d04-a273-4efe-93ec-133cccb836ff_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	male	control	2026-02-22 23:02:43.160793+00	\N
