ass="p-2 font-bold">tobacco</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">alcohol</td>\n                <td class="p-2">No</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">physical_activity</td>\n                <td class="p-2">3–4 days</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">diet_restrictions</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">support_network</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Question</td>\n                <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">generated_queries</td>\n                <td class="p-2">[\n  &quot;What types of traditional dances like umxhentso are popular for middle-aged men in urban areas of the Eastern Cape, and do they help improve fitness for people with diabetes and hypertension?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are the recommended exercise routines for a 45–54 year old male teacher living in a South African city with type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure?&quot;,\n  &quot;How does daily tobacco smoking affect blood pressure and diabetes management for Xhosa men in urban Eastern Cape communities?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any local support groups or wellness programs for men with diabetes and hypertension in urban centers of the Eastern Cape?&quot;,\n  &quot;What advice is there for incorporating exercise 3–4 days a week into the busy schedule of a working adult teacher in South Africa?&quot;,\n  &quot;Which healthy foods commonly available in urban Eastern Cape supermarkets are best for managing both diabetes and hypertension without restricting traditional isiXhosa meals?&quot;,\n  &quot;How do African Independent Churches in Eastern Cape approach physical wellness and offer community resources for members with chronic health conditions like mine?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there recommended physical activities or group events for middle-aged Xhosa men that combine social engagement and exercise in Eastern Cape cities?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can I balance traditional dancing and regular exercise to improve my cardiovascular health while managing insulin and oral diabetes medications?&quot;,\n  &quot;What practical steps can urban South African teachers with hypertension and diabetes take to reduce health risks while continuing daily smoking, or is quitting always necessary?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there culturally relevant strategies or stories from isiXhosa elders about maintaining health and exercise habits with chronic conditions in an urban environment?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can I find local healthcare providers in the Eastern Cape who are familiar with treating diabetes and hypertension in men from isiXhosa backgrounds?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">exercise</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">7268ef64-8a56-4fb6-8606-d94fe223e1fe</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">overlapped</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Let me tell you, my days as a teacher in the city are always busy. I wake up, make my vegan pap with morogo or samp and beans—healthy enough, you think?—rush to school, carry a flask of lemon water, and maybe fit in a smoke before lessons. But honestly, I never thought much about whether my skipping morning walks or dancing only at the weekend mattered for my health. Last week I had to organise a small spelling contest for my class. I was slow, sweating by midday, and my legs felt heavy. My head even spun a bit, and my voice was faint. I pushed through, but after class I asked myself: Am I just getting old, or is it something with my health?\n\nOn Friday I went to the local clinic nearby. The nurse, Sister Koyo, asked me about my routine, my dancing, the insulin and metformin. I told her, "Sisi, I do traditional dance, but only when I have the time. Is it enough?" She smiled and said, "Siyabonga, your dance is good, but you need more: at least 30 minutes of brisk walking, dancing, or cycling, five days a week—150 minutes total. You can break it into parts if you’re busy. Even snacking on exercise—short 10 minute walks—helps! Add strength moves, like squats, twice a week. It’s like ulwaluko—light work every day, not just when there’s a ceremony. You’ll feel stronger for your class and your family." She scribbled a plan on a paper and said, "Start small and ask your church or friends to join. And please, my brother, try to stop smoking—for your heart." I left feeling both nervous and hopeful.\n\nSo I tried her way—Monday, I walked the block before school. Tuesday, I asked my friend Vuyo to dance AmaMpondo steps with me in the yard. Wednesday, I was tired after marking, so I skipped the gym and felt guilty, but remembered her words: even small steps count. My thighs ached, but I kept moving, thinking of the big provincial competition I would help organise next month. What if I collapse in front of everyone? That pushed me to try again the next day.\n\nAfter a few weeks, I could feel my body getting lighter. My legs didn’t drag as much. Church friends even joined for short walks after evening prayers. By the time the competition came, I led the practices with jokes and energy. My students clapped for me, saying, "Sir, you’re younger!" I know now that, if I want to stay healthy and strong for my family and my work, these small steps can keep adding up. And who knows, maybe one day soon, I’ll be able to stop smoking too.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7268ef64-8a56-4fb6-8606-d94fe223e1fe_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7268ef64-8a56-4fb6-8606-d94fe223e1fe_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7268ef64-8a56-4fb6-8606-d94fe223e1fe_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7268ef64-8a56-4fb6-8606-d94fe223e1fe_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7268ef64-8a56-4fb6-8606-d94fe223e1fe_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7268ef64-8a56-4fb6-8606-d94fe223e1fe_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7268ef64-8a56-4fb6-8606-d94fe223e1fe_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7268ef64-8a56-4fb6-8606-d94fe223e1fe_3.mp3}	How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?	exercise	male	overlapped	2026-02-22 23:02:43.470547+00	\N
