        <td class="p-2">independent</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">BAD</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">crisis_avoidance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">crisis_avoidance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Yhoo, you know, every morning in Makhanda I wake up, pray, and get my kettle on for tea. I keep busy cleaning the house and making breakfast for myself—sometimes amagwinya, sometimes bread with jam. Snacking? Hei, I just take whatever is in the house, maybe the leftover chips or a fat cake from yesterday. I do my small laundry by hand outside, but today, while rinsing, I felt dizzy and had to sit down. Is it not just hungry or tired? Maybe it's nothing, I thought. But that little work, just the laundry, felt so hard. Could it be these snacks, or am I not doing something right?\n\nSo after that, I went to the clinic by the corner. Sister Zandile, she always has time, hey? She laughs and asks about my lunch. Kwazi bani, I tell her the truth. 'Mama Thandolwethu, you need to try snacks that give you energy, not just sugar and oil,' she says. 'Eat fruit, like apple or orange, or unsalted nuts—a handful, not too many. Even sliced cucumber with a little vinegar is good. Try snacking about twice a day—not all the time—but around 10am and 3pm, so your energy stays steady.' It sounded simple, but also a bit strange. Who eats cucumber for a snack? But I remembered my big Sunday cleaning was this weekend—maybe it's time to change little by little.\n\nBack at home, I tried to wash apples and slice cucumber. Hayi, old habits mos, I nearly reached for the leftover sweets, but I stopped. I had the apple instead. First days, I still felt like taking too many snacks, especially when I was bored, and almost missed my insulin because the routine was new. But then I remembered: big cleaning day is coming. I want to finish without feeling tired or dizzy like today. Maybe I must keep trying, just to see if it really helps.\n\nYou know what, Sunday arrived, and I went step by step. I took the right snacks at the right time—a handful of peanuts before mopping, then some orange after sweeping. The chores went much smoother! I was not so dizzy. I finished early and still had time for myself. Now, I think I can do this again next week! If eating better snacks can help me with all my work, then maybe I am ready to learn more healthy ways. Why should I not keep going, if just a little change makes the big tasks easier?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2fbf974e-c612-4231-8c58-981e84cf1d50_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2fbf974e-c612-4231-8c58-981e84cf1d50_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2fbf974e-c612-4231-8c58-981e84cf1d50_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2fbf974e-c612-4231-8c58-981e84cf1d50_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2fbf974e-c612-4231-8c58-981e84cf1d50_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2fbf974e-c612-4231-8c58-981e84cf1d50_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2fbf974e-c612-4231-8c58-981e84cf1d50_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2fbf974e-c612-4231-8c58-981e84cf1d50_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	female	independent	2026-02-22 23:02:43.781651+00	\N
