'You know, eating late at night can make your blood sugar rise in the morning and give you slow energy. It's better to eat your last meal at least 2 to 3 hours before bed, and choose lighter, healthy food, like spinach with pap or a piece of grilled fish with vegetables. This way, your sugar can stay steady, and you’ll feel less tired at school.' I nodded, but inside I thought, does it really make that much of a difference?\n\nThat night, I tried what Nurse Zanele said. I finished my marking earlier, and instead of the big plate of umngqusho I usually have at 10pm, I made a small portion at sunset—maize pap with spinach and tomato, just like she said. But yo, at 9pm my hands reached for the leftovers in the pot, thinking, 'Just a little bit won’t hurt.' Old habits, yhu! I stopped myself, remembering the big inter-schools cultural sports day was coming up next week, and I wanted to be sharp for my learners. It wasn’t easy. That first night, I lay in bed listening to the sounds outside, trying to ignore the small hunger biting my stomach.\n\nSlowly, day by day, I got used to eating earlier. Sometimes I slipped, but most nights I managed. On the morning of the big event, I woke up before my alarm, full of energy. I led my class in traditional games and even stayed after school to help clean up. My legs felt lighter, and I was more present—no yawning, no foggy head. I smiled, thinking, 'Maybe there’s something to this clinic advice after all.' My family and friends noticed, and now, even I am teaching others in church about eating earlier for strong health. Who would have thought, mpela, that a small change could make such a big difference in our village life?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/34b4103d-5262-4d6c-84b8-598f45a926e0_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/34b4103d-5262-4d6c-84b8-598f45a926e0_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/34b4103d-5262-4d6c-84b8-598f45a926e0_2.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/34b4103d-5262-4d6c-84b8-598f45a926e0_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/34b4103d-5262-4d6c-84b8-598f45a926e0_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/34b4103d-5262-4d6c-84b8-598f45a926e0_2.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	male	overlapped	2026-02-22 23:02:42.601733+00	\N
