o my class. You’d think a soccer fan like me would do it easily, right? Yoh, I started sweating, got short of breath, and even sat down in the corridor, hoping nobody would notice. It hit me—how can a young guy like me feel this tired from such a small thing?\n\nAt the clinic later that week, Sister Nomhle, the nurse, saw me sitting there with my head down. She asked me straight, "Siphosethu, are you moving your body between lessons, or do you wait for the weekend and church?" I shrugged, saying there’s no time, no gym nearby, and besides, I thought teaching is already active work. She just shook her head, told me, "It doesn’t have to be fancy. Walk up and down the staffroom during break, dance with your learners, or do simple stretches at home with your chair. Football teams around Gqeberha welcome social players—why not join for 20 minutes, just for fun, not to be Messi!" Honestly, it made sense but sounded... hard.\n\nI went home that evening with my thoughts somersaulting, wondering how to start. The next morning, I set a reminder to walk the corridor twice before break, but I forgot after marking books late. Still, the broken box memory was stuck in my mind, especially thinking about sports day next week, where all teachers coach learners outside. So, I pushed myself again, made it a challenge to do three slow squats while boiling the kettle, and a few stretches when collecting printouts. It was awkward, maybe even funny, but I told myself, 'Haibo, just try till Friday.'\n\nBy the weekend, I noticed a tiny change—I could carry the lesson plan box without feeling dizzy. On sports day, I joined the learners in a short football passing drill, breathless but smiling. Did I win the match? No, but I moved, laughed, and showed myself I can start somewhere. Now, every time I take my medicine or walk through school, I remember—small actions build strength, and one day I’ll join those social games in New Brighton with confidence. Who’s stopping me? Only myself!	{https://