"p-2 font-bold">tobacco</td>\n                <td class="p-2">No</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">alcohol</td>\n                <td class="p-2">No</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">physical_activity</td>\n                <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">diet_restrictions</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Kosher</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">support_network</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Question</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">generated_queries</td>\n                <td class="p-2">[\n  &quot;What are the best late-night snack options for a Xhosa woman with Type 2 diabetes living in an urban area of Eastern Cape, South Africa?&quot;,\n  &quot;How does eating after 9pm affect blood sugar control in women aged 35-44 with Type 2 diabetes who take both insulin and oral medication?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there traditional Xhosa foods that are suitable for managing diabetes when eaten in the evenings?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can I find diabetes-friendly exercise classes or physical activity groups for adults in urban Eastern Cape, especially for someone who enjoys traditional dancing like umxhentso?&quot;,\n  &quot;What resources or support groups exist in Eastern Cape urban areas for Baptist Christian women managing high alcohol consumption and Type 2 diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can I incorporate traditional dancing, such as intlombe, into my weekly routine to help manage my diabetes if I currently do not exercise?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are common cultural beliefs or approaches among isiXhosa women regarding using both traditional medicine and Western medication for diabetes management?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any local healthcare clinics in Eastern Cape cities offering nutrition counseling specifically for working adults with Type 2 diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are the risks of high alcohol intake for someone using both insulin and oral medication for diabetes in my age group?&quot;,\n  &quot;How does access to a variety of healthy foods in an Eastern Cape urban setting impact blood sugar control for women who work full-time and have diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;What practical strategies do other urban isiXhosa women with Type 2 diabetes use to avoid late-night eating after long workdays?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there faith-based diabetes support groups for Baptist Christians in Eastern Cape that could help with lifestyle changes and emotional support?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">exercise</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">7120b188-527c-4105-bd85-3f81c5f40411</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">consistency</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2">662f4d1a-53e7-4713-ae87-347de7e8cb76</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, sometimes I look at my calendar in the staff room at Walter Sisulu University and sigh. My days run together – teach, mark papers, grab a quick dinner, maybe scroll on my phone... Before I know it, it’s late, and I’m eating some samp and beans left over from lunch, maybe with too much maize. I tell myself, 'I will do traditional dancing again, next week.' But one night, while prepping the slides for my big research presentation, I felt so shaky and tired that I couldn’t finish. Is it just the work stress, or is something else going on with my health?\n\nThe next day, I stopped by the local clinic to refill my insulin, and Sister Nobuhle saw me waiting. She asked about my tiredness, and when I admitted the late-night snacks and no exercise, she didn’t scold. She said, 'Mam’Thobeka, sometimes diabetes can make you feel tired, but dancing—even a little bit—will help your insulin work better. Why not try dancing at home for ten minutes before supper? It can also help you keep your blood sugar steady so you don’t get those shaky moments when you work late. And remember, if you are hungry after 8pm, try yoghurt with a few berries, or carrots with hummus. You’ll manage your sugar and still enjoy your favourite foods.' I nodded but still felt a little unsure. Could it really be that simple?\n\nThat evening, I put on my favourite umxhentso song and tried the routines Sister Nobuhle suggested. At first, I forgot some steps and felt silly, especially when my daughter peeked in and giggled. Afterwards, I was tempted to go straight for the usual meal, but instead, I fixed a bowl of plain yoghurt with flaxseed like the nurse said. Later, working on my slides, I still felt tired, but less wobbly than before. There was a big faculty meeting coming, and I told myself—let me keep this up for the sake of that presentation.\n\nOver the week, the ten minutes of dancing became almost fun, and choosing healthy late-evening snacks got easier. On Friday, when my research presentation came, I felt steady, not shaky, and spoke with new confidence. When colleagues asked if I would show them the umxhentso steps, I smiled and agreed. Now, I’m not perfect, but I know small changes help. Maybe next time, I’ll even invite my work friends to join me—who knows, maybe they'll help me stay motivated too.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7120b188-527c-4105-bd85-3f81c5f40411_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7120b188-527c-4105-bd85-3f81c5f40411_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7120b188-527c-4105-bd85-3f81c5f40411_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7120b188-527c-4105-bd85-3f81c5f40411_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7120b188-527c-4105-bd85-3f81c5f40411_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7120b188-527c-4105-bd85-3f81c5f40411_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7120b188-527c-4105-bd85-3f81c5f40411_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7120b188-527c-4105-bd85-3f81c5f40411_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-02-22 23:02:43.581617+00	\N
