was spraying the maize, I suddenly felt tired and dizzy and nearly tripped over my own gumboots. Was it hunger? Or was it my sugar again? I just couldn’t finish the weeding—I had to rest under the old mango tree instead.\n\nLater that day, a nurse from the local clinic passed by, just as she does for us folks with diabetes. Ah, that woman knows her job! She smiled and asked, 'Odhiambo, have you eaten those sweet fried things again?' I laughed, a bit shy, and nodded. She shook her head, told me kindly that those sugary snacks just weren’t helping my work or my health. She said, 'Eh, you know, good snacking can be your friend! Try roasted groundnuts, fresh fruit like pawpaw or guava, or even a handful of boiled maize. No milk inside, just as you like. Snack small, three or four times, so your energy stays strong in the field. Ready to try?' I wanted to believe her, but could farm snacks really taste good?\n\nThe next day, I packed my bag with groundnuts and some slices of guava before heading out to weed the new field. At first, when the sun got high, I missed the hot samosa. But I remembered the nurse—her voice, her advice. After chewing some guava, I kept waiting for the sweet taste I was used to, but it was different, even strange. My stomach rumbled for mandazi, but there was a bigger field to finish before sunset. If I wanted to harvest well, I had to push through and trust my snacks would help me, even though my head was full of doubts.\n\nAs the afternoons passed, I started to notice something. My sugar was not making me so tired, and my hands didn’t shake when I carried the sprayer. One week later, I finished weeding both fields—first time in months I did it without sitting under the mango tree! My children clapped for me when they saw the neat rows. Now, every week, I try a new healthy snack—roasted groundnuts, boiled maize, or some bright fruit from the market. Who knew, eh? Maybe next time, I’ll teach my neighbour too. Farming is easier when you’re strong and your heart is happy.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/65e84a3a-f93d-4ba7-8514-8cee2d13c70a_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/65e84a3a-f93d-4ba7-8514-8cee2d13c70a_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/65e84a3a-f93d-4ba7-8514-8cee2d13c70a_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/65e84a3a-f93d-4ba7-8514-8cee2d13c70a_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/65e84a3a-f93d-4ba7-8514-8cee2d13c70a_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/65e84a3a-f93d-4ba7-8514-8cee2d13c70a_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/65e84a3a-f93d-4ba7-8514-8cee2d13c70a_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/65e84a3a-f93d-4ba7-8514-8cee2d13c70a_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	male	independent	2026-02-24 14:11:09.949211+00	\N
