es or foods that are safe and effective to use alongside insulin and Metformin for managing my blood sugar?&quot;,\n  &quot;What is the recommended diet plan for diabetic adults in Kenya, especially those who prefer locally available foods without major restrictions?&quot;,\n  &quot;How does alcohol consumption, even rarely, affect blood sugar control for someone with Type 2 diabetes in the context of Luo cultural events?&quot;,\n  &quot;Which animal products commonly kept in Kisumuâ€™s suburban households are beneficial or should be avoided for managing Type 2 diabetes?&quot;,\n  &quot;Is there any special guidance from Kisumu healthcare clinics about diabetes management for young male farmers like myself?&quot;,\n  &quot;What low-intensity physical activities can I incorporate into my daily routine as a farmer, considering my current lack of extra exercise days?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">diet</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">1cf3d67d-9266-445d-af3f-ae9fe12f2efd</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Which everyday foods can I eat that won&#x27;t make my blood sugar spike?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">independent</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Eh, every morning I wake up before daylight and head straight to my small shamba, thinking more of the soil and less about my breakfast. Honestly, what do I eat? Maybe some leftover ugali from yesterday, throw together a quick cup of sweet tea, then off I go. People always talk about healthy foods and diabetes, but does it really matter if you’re strong in the morning? Last Tuesday, I had to fix the wire by the chicken run—just a small job, but suddenly, my hands started shaking, and my legs turned jelly. That wire job left me sitting in the grass, sweating and confused. Was it all because of food or the sugar in my body? Who really knows?\n\nWhen I finally made it to the clinic, Nurse Atieno shook her head with that small smile, like she knows me better than I know myself. She asked, 'Odhiambo, have you tried eating sorghum ugali with greens and a bit of omena, or having sorghum porridge without sugar in the morning?' I laughed and said, 'Eh, those things are for old men!' But she didn’t give up—she showed me simple ways, like mixing soya in my ogi for breakfast and bringing roasted peanuts or guava for snacks instead of just chai. 'These won’t make your blood sugar bounce like a football,' she said. Does it really make such a big difference?\n\nI tried, really, I did. The next morning I made ogi with no sugar, just a little mashed plantain. By lunchtime, my tongue kept searching for that sweet taste, but I pushed on, thinking of my new plan to clear the old sorghum store—a most tiresome job coming next weekend. Still, my hands reached for those roasted peanuts instead of the usual mandazi. At first, I felt cross, like, why must I change everything now? But I knew the big field work would need all my strength, not just my stubbornness.\n\nBy the time the weekend came, I was feeling less tired and not so shaky in the arms. I worked the whole afternoon clearing the store, sweating under the sun but not out of breath or strength. Can you believe after all those small food changes, I managed the big task I used to dread? Now I finish the day with a soft smile, telling myself maybe this Nurse Atieno’s way is not so bad. Eh, I’m not done yet, but my mind is open. If good food makes my body strong for the farm, who am I to argue?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1cf3d67d-9266-445d-af3f-ae9fe12f2efd_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1cf3d67d-9266-445d-af3f-ae9fe12f2efd_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1cf3d67d-9266-445d-af3f-ae9fe12f2efd_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1cf3d67d-9266-445d-af3f-ae9fe12f2efd_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1cf3d67d-9266-445d-af3f-ae9fe12f2efd_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1cf3d67d-9266-445d-af3f-ae9fe12f2efd_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1cf3d67d-9266-445d-af3f-ae9fe12f2efd_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1cf3d67d-9266-445d-af3f-ae9fe12f2efd_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	male	independent	2026-02-24 14:11:10.838541+00	\N
