uot;,\n  &quot;Are there locally available, affordable healthy snacks in Kisumu that I can prepare to avoid eating late at night?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can Ohangla dancing be incorporated safely as exercise for young men with type 2 diabetes who are not currently active?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any resources in Kisumu for young adults with diabetes who do not smoke or drink and rely on close friends for support?&quot;,\n  &quot;Which Luo customs around food, celebrations, and fasting should I be aware of to better manage my diabetes in Kisumuâ€™s urban setting?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are the most common challenges faced by urban male farmers in Kisumu with type 2 diabetes, and how can they manage blood glucose during busy workdays?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">diet</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">ffd8547f-351f-4d74-bf7a-6e5b433ade8a</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Which everyday foods can I eat that won&#x27;t make my blood sugar spike?</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">independent</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">resistance</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Sometimes I wonder, is there really a difference in what I eat, or is it just work that makes everything feel hard? My day started early again—fields at dawn, shoulders aching from carrying sacks of sorghum to the market. Breakfast? Ehh, just a quick cup of strong, sweet uji from the street vendor. No time for real food, siwezi kubali kuchelewa kazi. But mid-morning, just as I started sorting the harvest, my hands started shaking, sweat on my forehead, heart beating fast. The sacks slipped from my grip and crashed down, scattering grain everywhere. Shame, boss stared. Did I not just eat? Why am I feeling weak like this?\n\nAt the clinic that afternoon, Sister Achieng’ sat me down and smiled, 'Omondi, let’s talk about what you are eating.' I tried to laugh it off, but inside I felt worried. She explained, “Your uji is good, but too much sugar is not your friend. You need energy, but not a spike. Try making sorghum porridge with cinnamon instead of sugar, eat some sukuma wiki and a boiled egg early, even if it feels slow. And, don’t wait until you feel faint. Small healthy snacks—groundnuts, sweet potatoes—won’t mess your blood sugar. Trust me, your body will thank you, especially when you have big work.” I nodded, half-sure, half-thinking, is it really that simple?\n\nThe next day, I managed to boil eggs and cook plain uji with ginger, no sugar. It felt weird, even tasted bland! My mind kept asking, can this keep me full? The old habits haunted me—market smells of mandazi and sweet tea. During break, friends joked, 'Omondi, kumbe wewe ni mzungu nowadays?' I almost gave up. But remembering I had a big delivery target the following week, I pushed on. I ate my boiled egg, chewed my groundnuts, and kept working, even if I wanted to just grab the sweet stuff.\n\nAs the week went by, I noticed something strange but good. I was less tired by midday, no more sudden shaking, and I moved more confidently through the busy market. Finally, delivery day came—I lifted those heavy sacks, loaded the cart, and made every bag count. My boss even smiled, ‘Sasa, Omondi, umekuwa strong man!’ Now I think, maybe this eating business really helps. Why not keep going? With each meal, it gets easier, and I start seeing food less as trouble, more as my friend.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ffd8547f-351f-4d74-bf7a-6e5b433ade8a_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ffd8547f-351f-4d74-bf7a-6e5b433ade8a_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ffd8547f-351f-4d74-bf7a-6e5b433ade8a_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ffd8547f-351f-4d74-bf7a-6e5b433ade8a_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ffd8547f-351f-4d74-bf7a-6e5b433ade8a_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ffd8547f-351f-4d74-bf7a-6e5b433ade8a_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ffd8547f-351f-4d74-bf7a-6e5b433ade8a_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ffd8547f-351f-4d74-bf7a-6e5b433ade8a_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	male	independent	2026-02-24 14:11:14.3575+00	\N
