eas of Kenya?&quot;,\n  &quot;Where can people in small rural communities near Kisumu find reliable and affordable fresh fruits and vegetables that fit a diabetic, Halal diet?&quot;,\n  &quot;Are there any mobile health clinics or outreach programs for diabetes and high blood pressure that serve rural traders in the Kisumu area?&quot;,\n  &quot;What strategies do other male traders in rural Luo communities use to stay motivated and manage exhaustion while balancing work, family, and chronic health conditions?&quot;,\n  &quot;Which types of drinks or alcoholic beverages common in the Kisumu region are safest or lowest in sugar for people with diabetes type 2 and hypertension?&quot;,\n  &quot;How does tobacco smoking affect diabetes and heart health for men in rural Kenya, and are there culturally accepted ways to reduce smoking in the Luo community?&quot;,\n  &quot;What are culturally acceptable ways in Luo Kenyan communities to engage family support networks in helping manage diabetes and stick to lifestyle changes?&quot;,\n  &quot;How can socialising with family and friends in small rural communities impact motivation and mental health for middle-aged men dealing with chronic conditions in Kisumu?&quot;\n]</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">Category</td>\n                <td class="p-2">diet</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">study_number</td>\n                <td class="p-2">73899845-4139-4d5a-a7cf-3d1735b7aeab</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">health_goals</td>\n                <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_format</td>\n                <td class="p-2">Text, Image and audio</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">type</td>\n                <td class="p-2">overlapped</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">parent</td>\n                <td class="p-2"></td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, some mornings I wake up with that heavy feeling in my legs, but I always tell myself, "Odhiambo, there is no time to slow down." Being a trader and small farmer in our village, I must start early to feed the chickens, check the goats, and get the freshest mboga for market. Still, to be honest, many nights I find myself sitting by the dim lantern, craving a plate of nyoyo or leftover ugali. Habits! "It’s just a little bite," I always think. One night last week, I stayed up to count the day's money, and by the time I slept, it was almost midnight. Next morning, eeh, I felt tired, and I reached the market late. My stall was not ready, and before I knew it, the best customers had passed my place. How many times now have I suffered just because of these late-night meals?\n\nOn clinic day, nurse Achieng’ took one look at me and shook her head, "Odhiambo, you look exhausted." I tried to laugh it off, saying, "Maybe too much rain last night, nurse." But inside, I knew. Then, she sat with me, not even in the examination room, just on that plastic bench under the shade. She explained, "Eating late can make your sugar high in the morning and make you tired. Why not eat your brown ugali and osuga with the children before dark, then just drink warm tea later if you are hungry? If you crave, take a little omena stew or fresh fruit, but not ugali at night. It will help your work in the market." At first, I didn’t want to change my habit, how could I work late without food to keep me company? But nurse Achieng’ spoke slowly, looking me in the eyes, like a father talking to son. Her word touched my heart.\n\nThat evening, as the sun was falling, I made sure to finish supper early with Mama and the children. I told myself, "Let me just try—Nurse Achieng’ wants to see me in full strength next market day." But as usual, around nine, my stomach rumbled and I walked to the kitchen, staring at the cold leftovers. I wanted to eat, so badly. Instead, I drank warm water with some lemon. The craving was still there, biting like a mosquito. I nearly gave in, but remembered tomorrow I had to go and buy a new weighing scale for my stall—a big purchase I couldn’t fail. If I was tired again, I wouldn’t get the best deal. So, I went back, sat with my last-born, and listened to radio stories till sleep found me.\n\nOver the next week, eating supper early became easier. My body felt stronger in the morning, and my hands stopped shaking after waking up. The day I went to the market for the weighing scale, I was sharp and full of ideas, standing tall, talking confidently with other traders. I finished my larger task without problems, and even brought home sweet bananas for the children. Now, I tell my friends, "You see, even us, we can change for better. It is hard in the beginning, but who said it must be easy?" I started looking forward to market days and even checked my blood sugar on nurse Achieng’s advice. I know it’s not magic, but now I see how small changes can open the road to a happier life.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/73899845-4139-4d5a-a7cf-3d1735b7aeab_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/73899845-4139-4d5a-a7cf-3d1735b7aeab_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/73899845-4139-4d5a-a7cf-3d1735b7aeab_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/73899845-4139-4d5a-a7cf-3d1735b7aeab_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/73899845-4139-4d5a-a7cf-3d1735b7aeab_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/73899845-4139-4d5a-a7cf-3d1735b7aeab_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/73899845-4139-4d5a-a7cf-3d1735b7aeab_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/73899845-4139-4d5a-a7cf-3d1735b7aeab_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	male	overlapped	2026-02-24 14:11:14.619773+00	\N
