a-a057-589130d8eb14</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">label</td>\n                <td class="p-2">EXCELLENT</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">story_arc</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n            <tr>\n                <td class="p-2 font-bold">output_tone</td>\n                <td class="p-2">social_support</td>\n            </tr>\n        \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	One Wednesday, I tried carrying a big box of books for marking to the staffroom. Easy, I thought. A small job! But I did not eat a good breakfast, and by the time I reached the staffroom, my hands shook, and I felt dizzy. It is strange, isn’t it? How can such a simple thing become so hard when you are not looking after your body? I just thought, maybe it’s tiredness, maybe I am not strong enough, or maybe I’m just growing old too fast.\n\nLater, I went to the local clinic to get my insulin. The nurse, Mama Atieno, saw me and asked, "Achieng, why do you look so worn out?" I told her about the box, and she explained with kindness: "You don’t need a gym to stay active, dear. Try marching on the spot, using water bottles as weights, or even dancing your Ohangla in your sitting room. Do these things every day, not just sometimes. Eat breakfast with some protein. It will help with your strength and your diabetes, ehee!" I doubted a bit—isn’t all this just dancing at home?\n\nThat evening, I tried what Mama Atieno said. I put on my favorite Ohangla, marched on the spot, and lifted bottled water like small dumbbells. I even tried some side steps. But, eish, after a few minutes, my arms felt heavy and I laughed at myself, wondering, am I sure I can keep this up? Halfway, I wanted to sit and watch TV, but I remembered those books at school, and that next week, I’d have to help with a bigger school project—arranging old library shelves. That made me go on, for just a few minutes more.\n\nLittle by little, it got easier. By Saturday, after just a few days, I was dancing longer, feeling less tired, and even waking up brighter. When the library task came, I moved books and shelves with more energy—and I didn’t feel dizzy! I realized it is not magic, just small clever changes, and it feels good. Maybe next, I’ll find a friend to join me at home. Now, I know even in this town flat, I can look after myself and do more than I ever thought.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e1bed1c2-acfe-46d2-aa16-f8cb0dd4eb02_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e1bed1c2-acfe-46d2-aa16-f8cb0dd4eb02_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e1bed1c2-acfe-46d2-aa16-f8cb0dd4eb02_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e1bed1c2-acfe-46d2-aa16-f8cb0dd4eb02_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e1bed1c2-acfe-46d2-aa16-f8cb0dd4eb02_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e1bed1c2-acfe-46d2-aa16-f8cb0dd4eb02_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e1bed1c2-acfe-46d2-aa16-f8cb0dd4eb02_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e1bed1c2-acfe-46d2-aa16-f8cb0dd4eb02_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-02-24 14:11:13.930328+00	\N
